{"reference":"Acts 20","book":"Acts","chapter":20,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"people_places","title":"A Multi-Regional Journey","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The named companions and the return through Macedonia portray a network extending across several cities and regions. The route from the coastal arrival at Neapolis to Philippi also fits the known Via Egnatia corridor. This makes the travel sequence geographically coherent, but the names alone do not establish that these travelers were official carriers of a collection.","verse_refs":["Acts 20:1-6","Acts 20:6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_020_64f45100a22d91f38ea58f99"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Miletus Speech","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul's address to the Ephesian elders gathers public and house-to-house teaching, tears, opposition, concern for the community, self-support, and warning of future danger into a farewell speech. It is best read as later narrative characterization of Paul rather than as a verbatim transcript or as proof that a community founded by a prominent leader could not face internal danger.","verse_refs":["Acts 20:17-38"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_020_44e67dd5d7eb06bbcac1bff2"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"In the speech, Paul connects his own work for his needs and those of his companions with helping the weak. The saying about giving is not found in the canonical Gospels. The passage presents self-support together with care for vulnerable people; it should not be turned into a universal demand for unpaid labor or a test against legitimate pay.","verse_refs":["Acts 20:17-38"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_020_ddfa6a1eac7b668cc1e95cd6"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The farewell ends with kneeling prayer, collective weeping, embraces, kisses, grief, and accompaniment to the ship. These actions show prayer and sorrow held together at a final parting, but an ancient narrated embrace does not override modern consent or prove universal reconciliation.","verse_refs":["Acts 20:36-38"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_020_c79f6e2c51874bef390fea93"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Journey and Jerusalem","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Pauline letters identify a major collection for Jerusalem, while Acts narrates this journey and its delegates without foregrounding the money; the significance of that omission is debated. For chronology, the direct letters should be considered first, with Acts functioning as a later narrative witness whose sequence, speeches, travel compression, and emphases should not silently replace the letters' own evidence.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_020_6a21bb38ca87986b50812536","reader_path_acts_020_a95fc23b3fcdaf948820ed78"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}